<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Training Transformers in Cosine Coefficient Space</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mohamed Amine Bergach
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">mbergach@illumina.com</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We parameterize the weight matrices of a transformer in the two-dimensional discrete cosine transform (DCT) domain, retaining only the lowest-frequency coefficients.
At each forward pass the full weight matrix is reconstructed via the inverse DCT; gradients propagate through the reconstruction to update the spectral coefficients directly.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">On character-level language modeling (Shakespeare, 1M characters), a 4-layer transformer trained from scratch in this representation matches the perplexity of the standard parameterization (6.1 vs. 6.1) while storing 52% of the parameters.
At 4<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> compression (29% of parameters), the model reaches perplexity 6.9—outperforming a low-rank baseline (perplexity 8.8 at 21% of parameters) at a comparable reduction.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.3" class="ltx_p">The method requires no architectural changes, no pre-trained checkpoint, and no auxiliary loss.
It reduces to replacing each <span id="abstract1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nn.Linear</span> with a drop-in spectral layer that stores <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> DCT coefficients instead of <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times m</annotation></semantics></math> weights.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The cost of training and deploying large language models is dominated by the size of their weight matrices.
A transformer with hidden dimension <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> layers stores <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(Ld^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(Ld^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> parameters, the majority in the linear projections for attention and feed-forward sub-layers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite>.
Reducing this count without sacrificing quality is a central problem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Two families of methods address it.
<em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Post-hoc compression</em> (pruning, quantization, distillation) trains a full model first, then reduces it.
<em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Structured parameterization</em> constrains the weight representation during training itself, so that the model is born small: low-rank factorization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, butterfly matrices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and spectral methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koutník et al., 2010</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rippel et al., 2015</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work has shown that spectral representations are effective for <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fine-tuning</em> pre-trained transformers.
FourierFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> parameterizes weight updates as sparse DFT coefficients; sDCTFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen and others, 2025</a>)</cite> uses the discrete cosine transform with stratified frequency selection.
Both achieve strong results with far fewer trainable parameters than LoRA.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These fine-tuning methods start from a pre-trained checkpoint, which already encodes the bulk of the model’s knowledge.
A natural question is whether spectral parameterization works for <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pre-training from scratch</em>, where no such prior exists.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koutník et al. (2010)</a></cite> showed this for small networks trained with evolutionary algorithms; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Irie and Schmidhuber (2021)</a></cite> extended it to recurrent networks with gradient descent.
Neither tested transformers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We show that a standard transformer can be pre-trained from scratch with its weight matrices parameterized in the 2D DCT domain, matching full-parameter quality at 2<math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> compression and maintaining competitive quality at 4<math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>.
The approach requires no changes to the model architecture, no pre-trained initialization, and no auxiliary regularization loss.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Weight Parameterization via DCT</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A standard linear layer computes <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}=W\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐛</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=W\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{b}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mn</annotation></semantics></math> trainable parameters.
We replace <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> with a reconstruction from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> DCT coefficients:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=\text{IDCT-2D}(\tilde{C}),\quad\tilde{C}_{ij}=\begin{cases}c_{k}&amp;\text{if }(i,j)=f_{k}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>IDCT-2D</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=\text{IDCT-2D}(\tilde{C}),\quad\tilde{C}_{ij}=\begin{cases}c_{k}&amp;\text{if }(i,j)=f_{k}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(f_{k},c_{k})\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(f_{k},c_{k})\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> are the <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> retained frequency indices and their real-valued amplitudes, and IDCT-2D is the two-dimensional inverse discrete cosine transform (type II, orthonormal).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The trainable parameters are the <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> amplitudes <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1},\ldots,c_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1},\ldots,c_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The bias <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}</annotation></semantics></math> is stored conventionally.
The total parameter count for the layer is <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K+m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K+m</annotation></semantics></math> (amplitudes plus bias), compared to <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mn+m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mn+m</annotation></semantics></math> for a standard layer.
For <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=mn/r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=mn/r</annotation></semantics></math>, this is an <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\times</annotation></semantics></math> compression.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Frequency Selection</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We retain the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> lowest-frequency coefficients, ordered by a zigzag scan of the 2D frequency grid (the same ordering used in JPEG compression).
Specifically, indices <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> are sorted by <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+j</annotation></semantics></math> (with ties broken by alternating direction), and the first <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> are retained.
This prioritizes smooth, large-scale patterns over high-frequency detail.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The frequency indices are fixed before training and do not change.
Adaptive selection (learning which frequencies to retain) is possible but adds complexity; we find the fixed low-frequency selection sufficient.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Forward and Backward Pass</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The forward pass reconstructs <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> from the <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> coefficients via Eq. <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Weight Parameterization via DCT ‣ 2 Method ‣ Training Transformers in Cosine Coefficient Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and then computes the matrix–vector product as usual.
The reconstruction costs <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(mn\log(mn))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(mn\log(mn))</annotation></semantics></math> via the fast DCT, compared to <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(mn)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(mn)</annotation></semantics></math> for accessing a stored weight matrix.
The logarithmic overhead is modest and does not dominate the forward pass for the model sizes we test.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Backpropagation through the IDCT is straightforward: the DCT is a linear operator, so</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial c_{k}}=\left[\text{DCT-2D}\!\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial W}\right)\right]_{f_{k}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:3.884em;" width="3.884em"><mtext>DCT-2D</mtext></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial c_{k}}=\left[\text{DCT-2D}\!\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial W}\right)\right]_{f_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The gradient with respect to each coefficient is the value of the 2D DCT of the weight gradient at the corresponding frequency index.
This projects the full <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mn</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional gradient onto the <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional spectral subspace.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Initialization</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We initialize the <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> coefficients with i.i.d. Gaussian noise scaled by</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=\sqrt{\frac{2}{n}}\cdot\sqrt{\frac{mn}{K}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>K</mi></mfrac></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=\sqrt{\frac{2}{n}}\cdot\sqrt{\frac{mn}{K}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The first factor is the Kaiming scale for a ReLU/GELU network; the second compensates for the reduced degrees of freedom so that the reconstructed weight matrix has approximately the same variance as a Kaiming-initialized dense matrix.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Setup</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train a character-level language model on the <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tinyshakespeare</span> dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karpathy, 2015</a>)</cite>: 1M characters of Shakespeare’s collected works, split 90/10 into training and validation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Architecture.</span>
4 layers, 128-dimensional embeddings, 4 attention heads, context length 128.
All linear layers in attention (QKV projection, output projection) and the feed-forward network (two layers with GELU activation) are parameterized via the method under test.
The token and position embeddings and the final language model head use standard dense parameters in all configurations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training.</span>
30 epochs, AdamW optimizer with weight decay 0.01, cosine learning rate schedule.
Batch size 32, gradient clipping at norm 1.0.
Learning rate: <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> for standard and low-rank models, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> for DCT models (the spectral parameterization benefits from a moderately larger step size, consistent with observations in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2024)</a></cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
(1) <em id="S3.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Standard</em>: all linear layers are dense <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nn.Linear</span>.
(2) <em id="S3.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Low-rank</em> (LoRA-style): each <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is factored as <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AB</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, with rank <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=16</annotation></semantics></math>.
Unlike the original LoRA, we train <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> from scratch (no pre-trained base model).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Results</h3>

<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Character-level language modeling on <span id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tinyshakespeare</span>.
Perplexity (lower is better) on the validation set after 30 epochs.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Method</th>
<th id="S3.T1.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Parameters</th>
