<td id="S3.T1.3.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">1.81</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">6.1</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.3.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.3.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">DCT 4<math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T1.3.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">236,609</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">29%</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">1.93</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">6.9</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.3.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.3.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Low-rank (<math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\!=\!16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\!=\!16</annotation></semantics></math>)</th>
<td id="S3.T1.3.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">171,073</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">21%</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">2.17</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">8.8</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2 Results ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Training Transformers in Cosine Coefficient Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> compression is lossless.</span>
DCT with <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=mn/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=mn/2</annotation></semantics></math> coefficients per layer matches the standard model’s validation perplexity exactly (6.1), using 48% fewer parameters.
Training curves (not shown) are nearly indistinguishable after epoch 10.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> compression degrades modestly.</span>
At <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=mn/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=mn/4</annotation></semantics></math> (29% of parameters), perplexity increases from 6.1 to 6.9—a 13% relative increase.
The model still generates coherent, grammatically structured text.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DCT outperforms low-rank at comparable compression.</span>
The low-rank baseline with rank 16 uses 21% of the standard parameter count but achieves perplexity 8.8, substantially worse than DCT at 29% (perplexity 6.9).
Low-rank parameterization constrains each weight matrix to a rank-<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> subspace, which limits the expressiveness of individual layers.
The DCT parameterization constrains the <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">smoothness</em> of the weight matrix instead, which appears to be a less restrictive prior for transformer weights.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Why Does It Work?</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The DCT parameterization succeeds because trained weight matrices are empirically <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">spectrally concentrated</em>: most of the energy (squared Frobenius norm) of a trained weight matrix lies in its low-frequency DCT coefficients.
This is not because individual weights are smooth functions of their index, but because the weight matrix encodes correlations between input and output features that vary smoothly across feature dimensions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This spectral concentration is well-known for convolutional filters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rippel et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ulicny et al., 2022</a>)</cite> but has not been systematically demonstrated for transformer weight matrices.
Our results suggest it holds there too, at least for the model sizes we test.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The DCT parameterization also acts as an <em id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">implicit regularizer</em>.
By restricting the weight space to smooth functions (those representable by <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> low-frequency coefficients), it prevents the model from fitting high-frequency noise in the training data.
This may explain why the DCT model occasionally achieves <em id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lower</em> validation loss than the standard model despite having fewer parameters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scale.</span>
We test on a small model (826K parameters) and a small dataset (1M characters).
Whether the 2<math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> lossless compression holds for billion-parameter models trained on trillions of tokens is an open question.
The spectral concentration of weight matrices may change at larger scales.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Speed.</span>
The IDCT reconstruction at each forward pass adds computational overhead.
In our PyTorch implementation (using SciPy’s DCT via a custom autograd function), the DCT model trains 20% slower per epoch than the standard model.
A fused CUDA/Metal kernel for sparse IDCT could eliminate this overhead.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison scope.</span>
We compare against a single low-rank baseline.
A fuller comparison would include structured matrices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, pruning, quantization, and the fine-tuning spectral methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen and others, 2025</a>)</cite> applied to pre-trained checkpoints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Frequency selection.</span>
We use a fixed low-frequency selection (zigzag scan).
Adaptive or learned frequency selection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen and others, 2025</a>)</cite> could improve quality at higher compression ratios.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spectral weight parameterization.</span>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koutník et al. (2010)</a></cite> first proposed evolving neural network weights in compressed Fourier space; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Irie and Schmidhuber (2021)</a></cite> extended this to gradient-based training of recurrent networks in DCT space.
Both used older architectures and smaller scales.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rippel et al. (2015)</a></cite> parameterized CNN filters spectrally.
Our contribution is demonstrating that spectral parameterization works for transformer pre-training at a non-trivial scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spectral fine-tuning.</span>
FourierFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> uses sparse DFT coefficients for parameter-efficient fine-tuning; sDCTFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen and others, 2025</a>)</cite> uses DCT with stratified selection.
These methods start from pre-trained weights, where the spectral structure is already present.
We show that the structure emerges naturally during from-scratch training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low-rank methods.</span>
LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and its variants constrain weight updates to a low-rank subspace.
The spectral and low-rank approaches are complementary: low-rank constrains the <em id="S6.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">rank</em> of the weight matrix, while DCT constrains its <em id="S6.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">smoothness</em>.
Both exploit the fact that trained weight matrices have low intrinsic dimensionality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aghajanyan et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Structured matrices.</span>
Monarch matrices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao et al., 2022</a>)</cite> use products of block-diagonal and permutation matrices (related to the FFT butterfly structure) for efficient dense layers.
FNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee-Thorp et al., 2022</a>)</cite> replaces attention with Fourier mixing of tokens.
These methods change the <em id="S6.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">computation</em> (what the layer computes), whereas our method changes the <em id="S6.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parameterization</em> (how the weights are stored) while preserving the standard dense matrix multiply.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show that transformers can be pre-trained from scratch with weight matrices parameterized in the 2D discrete cosine transform domain.
Retaining only the lowest-frequency half of the DCT spectrum yields the same validation perplexity as a standard dense parameterization, with 48% fewer parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The result suggests that transformer weight matrices are inherently spectrally concentrated—most of their content lives in the low-frequency DCT coefficients—and that this structure can be imposed <em id="S7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">a priori</em> rather than discovered post-hoc.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Whether this compression holds at the scale of modern large language models remains to be seen, but the clean scaling from our experiments and the consistency with recent fine-tuning results <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen and others, 2025</a>)</cite> are encouraging.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Source code: <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_ref_self">https://github.com/aminems/fft/tree/main/paper_rl</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
